(637148, 62783, 1031, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by scalvert:</b></div><hr/><i> Good idea, but wouldn''t the flames be trailing an [upward] moving ball? Just turn the ball upside down before shooting and rotate the image 180 degrees to get the effect. A low-speed fan on the flames would really give the impression of a meteor. </i></td></tr></table> <br/>If anything, I''d rotate it clockwise, so the flames trail horizontally. As someone pointed out, a common baseball term for a fastball is "heater."</td>', 'Originally posted by scalvert: Good idea, but wouldn''t the flames be trailing an [upward] moving ball? Just turn the ball upside down before shooting and rotate the image 180 degrees to get the effect. A low-speed fan on the flames would really give the impression of a meteor.  If anything, I''d rotate it clockwise, so the flames trail horizontally. As someone pointed out, a common baseball term for a fastball is "heater."', '2004-03-25 00:03:16.000000', NULL, 0),
